{"name":"Generic Viridian","hex":"#007f66","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"generic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very broad; pertaining or appropriate to large classes or groups as opposed to specific instances.","Lacking in precision, often in an evasive fashion; vague; imprecise."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A product sold under a generic name.","A wine that is a combination of several wines, or made from a combination of several grape varieties."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/generic"},{"term":"viridian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bluish-green pigment made from chromium sesquioxide.","A bluish-green color."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a bluish green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/viridian"}],"entries":[{"title":"Viridian","blocks":[{"text":"Viridian is a blue-green pigment, a hydrated chromium(III) oxide, of medium saturation and relatively dark in value. It is composed of a majority of green, followed by blue. The first recorded use of viridian as a color name in English was in the 1860s. Viridian takes its name from the Latin viridis, meaning \"green\". The pigment was first prepared in mid-19th-century Paris and remains available from several US manufacturers as prepared artists' colors in all media."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Chromium%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg/330px-Chromium%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viridian","wikidata":"Q6163776"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Viridian%28Pigment_Green_18%29_%28Left%29_And_Phthalocyanine_Green%28Pigment_Green_7%29_%28Right%29_Gouache.jpg/500px-Viridian%28Pigment_Green_18%29_%28Left%29_And_Phthalocyanine_Green%28Pigment_Green_7%29_%28Right%29_Gouache.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser